description:
This test validates the string representation behavior of ToStringBuilder when appending Map objects with different formatting options, specifically testing how empty and single-entry maps are rendered in both compact and detailed formats. Initialize a ToStringBuilder instance with a base Integer object and call the append method with the field name "a", an empty Map created using Collections.emptyMap(), and a boolean false parameter to test compact formatting, then assert that the resulting string matches the expected baseStr concatenated with "[a=<size=0>]". Create another ToStringBuilder with the same base object and call append with field name "a", the same empty Map, but with boolean true for detailed formatting, asserting the output equals baseStr plus "[a=
    {
    }
    ]" which shows the multi-line expanded format. Construct a third ToStringBuilder with the base object and append field "a" with a single-entry Map created using Collections.singletonMap("k", "v") and false for compact mode, verifying the result matches baseStr concatenated with "[a=<size=1>]". Finally, instantiate a fourth ToStringBuilder with the base object and call append with field "a", the same single-entry Map, and true for detailed formatting, asserting the output equals baseStr plus "[a=
    {
        k=v
    }
    ]" to validate the expanded multi-line representation with proper indentation. The test uses private final fields base of type Integer and baseStr of type String from the test class. In the teardown method, call ToStringBuilder.setDefaultStyle with ToStringStyle.DEFAULT_STYLE to reset the default styling configuration. This test uses the JUnit testing framework.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when escape characters are present in template strings, specifically testing how the StrSubstitutor class handles dollar sign escaping and variable replacement across multiple input formats. Initialize a HashMap field called values in the test class setup with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog", then call the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: the expected result string "The $
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog.", the template string "The $$
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    .", and the boolean value true to enable substring testing. The helper method creates a StrSubstitutor instance initialized with the values map, then systematically tests the replace method across six different input types: String, character array, StringBuffer, StringBuilder, StrBuilder, and MutableObject, asserting that each returns the expected result string where escaped dollar signs become single dollar signs and variable placeholders are replaced with their corresponding values from the map. For each input type when substring testing is enabled, the helper method also tests substring replacement by calling replace with start index 1 and end index at length minus 2, asserting the result equals the expected result with the first and last characters removed. Additionally, the helper method tests in-place replacement using replaceIn methods on StringBuffer, StringBuilder, and StrBuilder objects, asserting that each replaceIn call returns true indicating a replacement occurred and that the modified object's string representation matches the expected result. In the teardown phase, set the values field to null to clean up resources. This test uses the JUnit testing framework as indicated by the @Test annotation.

This test validates the complex escaping functionality of string substitution when variable placeholders contain nested escape sequences and undefined variables with default values. The test exercises the StrSubstitutor class's ability to handle intricate template patterns with multiple levels of escaping and verifies that both full string replacement and substring replacement operations produce correct results across various input types. Initialize a HashMap field named values in the test class setup with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog". Execute a helper method twice with different parameter sets to test complex escaping scenarios. In the first invocation, pass an expected result string "The $
    {
        quick brown fox
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog.", a template string containing nested escaped placeholders "The $$
    {
        $
        {
            animal
        }
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    .", and a boolean value true to enable substring testing. In the second invocation, use a similar pattern but include an undefined variable with a default value, passing the expected result "The $
    {
        quick brown fox
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog. $
    {
        1234567890
    }
    ." and template "The $$
    {
        $
        {
            animal
        }
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    . $$
    {
        $
        {
            undefined.number:-1234567890
        }
    }
    ." with true for substring testing. The helper method creates a StrSubstitutor instance with the values map, calculates an expected short result by removing the first and last characters, then systematically tests the replace method across multiple input types including String, char array, StringBuffer, StringBuilder, StrBuilder, and MutableObject, asserting that each produces the expected result using assertEquals. When substring is true, the helper additionally tests substring replacement operations using overloaded replace methods with start index 1 and end index length minus 2, asserting the shortened expected result. The helper also validates in-place replacement using replaceIn methods on StringBuffer, StringBuilder, and StrBuilder instances, asserting that the operations return true and the modified objects contain the expected result string. Clean up by setting the values field to null in the teardown method. This test uses the JUnit testing framework as indicated by the Test annotation.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when handling incomplete variable prefix patterns in template strings, specifically testing the StrSubstitutor class's ability to process templates with malformed variable references and perform partial string replacements. Initialize a HashMap field called values in the test class setup with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog", then call the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: an expected result string "The
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog.", a replacement template "The
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    .", and the boolean value true to enable substring testing. The helper method creates a StrSubstitutor instance initialized with the values map, then performs comprehensive testing across multiple data types including String, character array, StringBuffer, StringBuilder, StrBuilder, and MutableObject by calling the replace method on each type and asserting the result equals the expected string. When substring testing is enabled, the helper method also tests partial replacement by calling replace with start index 1 and end index at length minus 2, expecting a shortened version of the expected result with the first and last characters removed. Additionally, the helper method tests in-place replacement using replaceIn methods on mutable types like StringBuffer, StringBuilder, and StrBuilder, asserting that these methods return true to indicate successful replacement and that the modified objects contain the expected result string. In the teardown phase, set the values field to null to clean up resources. The testing framework used is JUnit, identified by the @Test annotation.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when variable placeholders have prefix delimiters but no suffix delimiters in template strings, specifically testing the StrSubstitutor class's ability to handle malformed variable syntax and perform substring operations on various string-like data structures. Initialize a HashMap field called values in the test class setup with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog", then call the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: an expected result string containing "The $
{
animal jumps over the $
{
target
}
lazy dog.", a template string with malformed variable syntax "The $
{
animal jumps over the $
{
target
}
$
{
target
}
.", and a boolean value true to enable substring testing. The helper method creates a StrSubstitutor instance initialized with the values map, then systematically tests the replace method across multiple data types including String, char array, StringBuffer, StringBuilder, StrBuilder, and MutableObject, asserting that each returns the expected result string, and when substring is true, also tests substring operations by calling replace with start index 1 and end index length minus 2, expecting a shortened version of the expected result with first and last characters removed. Additionally, the helper method tests in-place replacement using replaceIn methods on StringBuffer, StringBuilder, and StrBuilder instances, asserting that each replaceIn call returns true and that the modified buffer contents match the expected result string, with substring variants following the same pattern of testing partial replacement within specified bounds. In the teardown, set the values field to null to clean up resources. This test uses the JUnit testing framework as indicated by the @Test annotation.

Initialize a string input containing multiple tokens separated by semicolons and newlines, including quoted sections with escaped quotes, where the input is "a;
    b;
    c;
    "d;
    ""e";
    f;
    ;
    ;
    ". Create a new StringTokenizer instance with this input string, then configure the tokenizer by calling setDelimiterChar with a semicolon and newline character as the delimiter, setQuoteChar with a double quote character to handle quoted sections, setIgnoredMatcher with the result of calling noneMatcher on the INSTANCE field of StringMatcherFactory to specify no characters should be ignored, and setIgnoreEmptyTokens with false to ensure empty tokens are preserved in the output. Call the getTokenArray method on the StringTokenizer to retrieve the parsed tokens as a string array, which internally triggers the tokenization process that splits the input based on the configured delimiters while respecting quoted sections and handling escaped quotes within them. Define an expected array containing the exact tokens that should result from parsing: "a", "b", " c", "d;
        "e", "f", " ", " ", and an empty string. Assert that the length of the returned tokens array equals the length of the expected array using assertEquals with the tokens array converted to string format for error messaging. Iterate through each index of the expected array and assert that each token at the corresponding index in the returned array matches the expected value using assertEquals, providing detailed error messages that include the index position, actual token value, and expected token value for debugging purposes. This test validates the StringTokenizer's ability to correctly parse complex input containing semicolon delimiters, newline characters, quoted sections with embedded delimiters, escaped quotes, and empty tokens while preserving whitespace and handling edge cases. The testing framework used is JUnit, identified by the @Test annotation.

Initialize a StringTokenizer instance with a multi-line input string containing semicolon-delimited tokens including quoted sections with embedded semicolons and newlines, specifically "a;
    b;
    c;
    "d;
    ""e";
    f;
    ;
    ;
    ". Configure the tokenizer by calling setDelimiterChar with a semicolon followed by a newline character, setQuoteChar with a double quote character, setIgnoredMatcher with the trimMatcher from the StringMatcherFactory INSTANCE field of the test class which is a public static final StringMatcherFactory, and setIgnoreEmptyTokens with false to ensure empty tokens are preserved. Call getTokenArray to retrieve the parsed tokens as a String array, then create an expected array containing "a", "b", " c", "d;
        "e", "f", and three null values representing empty tokens. Iterate through all tokens using hasNext and next methods while counting iterations, then iterate backwards through all tokens using hasPrevious and previous methods while counting those iterations. Assert that the length of the tokens array equals the length of the expected array using assertEquals with Arrays.toString of tokens as the failure message, assert that the forward iteration count equals the expected array length with the message "could not cycle through entire token list using the 'hasNext' and 'next' methods", and assert that the backward iteration count equals the expected array length with the message "could not cycle through entire token list using the 'hasPrevious' and 'previous' methods". This test validates that the StringTokenizer correctly parses complex delimited text with quotes, embedded delimiters, whitespace handling, and bidirectional iteration capabilities. The testing framework used is JUnit, identified by the @Test annotation.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when a variable replacement results in an identical output to the original template, specifically testing the StrSubstitutor class's ability to handle circular or self-referential variable definitions without infinite recursion. Initialize a HashMap called values in the setUp method with two key-value pairs: "animal" mapped to "quick brown fox" and "target" mapped to "lazy dog", then in the test method, override the "animal" key with a complex multi-line string containing nested variable references and braces, and set "thing" to "animal" to create a circular reference pattern. Call the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: the expected result string "The $
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps.", the same string as the replacement template, and the boolean value true to indicate substring matching should be enabled. The helper method creates a new StrSubstitutor instance initialized with the values map and delegates to another doTestReplace overload that performs the actual substitution testing and validation. In the tearDown method, set the values field to null to clean up the test state. This test uses the JUnit testing framework as indicated by the @Test annotation.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when variable placeholders lack proper prefix and suffix delimiters, ensuring the substitution mechanism correctly handles malformed template syntax. The test exercises the StrSubstitutor class's replace method with a template containing improperly formatted variable references and asserts that the substitution behavior matches expected outcomes for malformed syntax scenarios. Initialize a HashMap field named values in the test class setup method, populating it with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog". Execute the test method annotated with @Test by calling the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: the expected result string "The animal
}
jumps over the lazy dog.", the replacement template string "The animal
}
jumps over the $
{
    target
}
.", and the boolean value true. Within the helper method, create a new StrSubstitutor instance initialized with the values map, then call another overloaded doTestReplace method passing the StrSubstitutor instance along with the expected result, template, and boolean parameters to perform the actual substitution validation and assertion logic. In the teardown method, set the values field to null to clean up the test state. The test class is annotated with @Deprecated and uses standard JUnit testing framework.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when processing templates with incomplete variable prefix syntax, specifically testing that the StrSubstitutor class correctly handles malformed variable references and performs partial replacements as expected. Initialize a HashMap field called values in the setUp method, populating it with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog", which serve as the variable substitution dictionary for template processing. Execute the testReplaceIncompletePrefix method annotated with @Test, which calls the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: an expected result string containing "The
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog.", a replacement template string "The
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    .", and a boolean value true indicating substring processing should be enabled. The helper method doTestReplace creates a new StrSubstitutor instance initialized with the values map, then delegates to an overloaded doTestReplace method that performs the actual substitution operation and validates that the incomplete prefix syntax (using $ instead of proper variable delimiters) results in the expected partial replacement behavior where only properly formatted variables are substituted while malformed ones remain unchanged. In the tearDown method, set the values field to null to clean up the test state. This test uses the standard JUnit testing framework as indicated by the @Test annotation.

This test validates the string substitution functionality when escape characters are used to prevent variable replacement, specifically testing that dollar signs can be escaped to avoid unwanted substitution while still allowing intended variable replacements to occur. Initialize a HashMap field called values in the test class setup with two key-value pairs: "animal" mapped to "quick brown fox" and "target" mapped to "lazy dog". Execute the testReplaceEscaping method which calls the helper method doTestReplace with three parameters: the expected result string "The $
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog.", a replacement template string "The $$
    {
        animal
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    .", and a boolean value true. The helper method creates a new StrSubstitutor instance using the values map and delegates to another doTestReplace method to perform the actual substitution testing, where the StrSubstitutor processes the template string by escaping the double dollar sign to a single dollar sign while replacing the target variable placeholder with "lazy dog" from the values map, validating that the escape mechanism works correctly to produce the expected output. In the teardown phase, set the values field to null to clean up resources. This test uses the standard JUnit testing framework as indicated by the @Test annotation.

This test validates the complex escaping functionality of string substitution when processing templates with nested variable references and escape sequences. The test specifically examines the StrSubstitutor class and its ability to handle escaped dollar signs and nested variable placeholders, asserting that the substitution produces the expected output strings. Initialize a HashMap field named values in the setUp method, populating it with key-value pairs where "animal" maps to "quick brown fox" and "target" maps to "lazy dog". Execute the testReplaceComplexEscaping method which calls a helper method doTestReplace twice with different parameter sets. In the first call, pass the expected result string "The $
    {
        quick brown fox
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog.", the template string "The $$
    {
        $
        {
            animal
        }
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    .", and the boolean value true. In the second call, pass a similar expected result string that includes an additional undefined number reference "The $
    {
        quick brown fox
    }
    jumps over the lazy dog. $
    {
        1234567890
    }
    .", the corresponding template with escaped sequences "The $$
    {
        $
        {
            animal
        }
    }
    jumps over the $
    {
        target
    }
    . $$
    {
        $
        {
            undefined.number:-1234567890
        }
    }
    .", and the boolean value true. The helper method doTestReplace creates a new StrSubstitutor instance using the values map and delegates to another doTestReplace method to perform the actual substitution validation. In the tearDown method, set the values field to null to clean up resources. The test uses the @Test annotation and the test class is marked with @Deprecated.
